{"type": "mycroft.session.set", "namespace": "visage-templates", "data": {"text": "A teaspoon is about five millilitres, a tablespoon is three teaspoons, and a cup is sixteen tablespoons. So a cup holds forty-eight teaspoons, or roughly two hundred and forty millilitres. In the United Kingdom the cup was historically larger, about two hundred and eighty-four millilitres, but most modern recipes use the metric cup of two hundred and fifty millilitres. When a recipe says a scant cup it means slightly less than full; a heaping tablespoon is mounded above the rim. Dry ingredients should be levelled with a knife, and flour should be spooned into the cup rather than scooped."}}
